# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
# setuptools' scratch tree, left behind by `pip install .` on some pip versions.
/build/

# Generated datasets, metric tables and rendered reports.  All reproducible from a
# manifest and a seed, so the manifest is what gets versioned, not the data.
# A curated dataset is different: it lives in simulator/providers/curated/ and IS committed.
# Anchored to the root: an unanchored `data/` matches a directory of that name at any
# depth, which also swallowed project_metadata/data/ — the committed scenario file the
# explainer figures are generated from (9.1).
/data/
*.npy

# Deliberately NOT ignored: simulator/gui/static/formulation.html.  It is a committed
# pre-render, and shipping it is what lets a default install serve the GUI (10.3.10).
# Everything the GUI writes at runtime goes under data/ and is covered above.

# Execution plans.  A plan is a transient working artifact — it tracks progress through
# one piece of work and is stale the moment that work lands.  It may be persisted here
# so a run survives a session, but it is never versioned and never cited as a source of
# requirements; the spec is what carries those forward.
project_metadata/plans/

# Agent tooling state and per-developer editor config.  None of it is a product artifact,
# and `.superpowers/` holds live session state — pids, logs, ports and a server token —
# which must never reach a public repository.  `docs/superpowers/` is the same category of
# transient plan that `project_metadata/plans/` above is already excluded for.
.superpowers/
docs/superpowers/
.cursor/
.agents/

# Project-scope skills are installed per machine, not shipped.  `.claude/skills/` here is
# symlinks into the ignored `.agents/` tree above, so committing them would hand a fresh
# clone four broken links.  `skills-lock.json` is the installer's own reinstall manifest
# for that same tree.
.claude/skills/
skills-lock.json

# draw.io desktop atomic-write temp files
.$*.drawio.dtmp
